Presidential elections were held in [[Timor-Leste]] on 19 March 2022.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|date=2022-01-23|title=East Timor Nobel laureate Ramos-Horta to run for president again|url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20220123-east-timor-nobel-laureate-ramos-horta-to-run-for-president-again|access-date=2022-01-23|website=France 24|language=en|archive-date=30 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220130000757/https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20220123-east-timor-nobel-laureate-ramos-horta-to-run-for-president-again|url-status=live}}</ref> Incumbent [[Francisco Guterres]] sought election to a second term.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Barrett|first=Chris|date=2021-12-19|title='Thousands pleading with me to run': Jose Ramos-Horta eyes return as East Timor president|url=https://www.smh.com.au/world/asia/thousands-pleading-with-me-to-run-jose-ramos-horta-eyes-return-as-east-timor-president-20211216-p59i6o.html|access-date=2022-01-11|website=The Sydney Morning Herald|language=en|archive-date=13 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220113093141/https://www.smh.com.au/world/asia/thousands-pleading-with-me-to-run-jose-ramos-horta-eyes-return-as-east-timor-president-20211216-p59i6o.html|url-status=live}}</ref> As none of the presidential nominees received at least 50% of the cast votes, a [[Two-round system|runoff]] was held on 19 April 2022, between the top two candidates, [[José Ramos-Horta]] and Guterres.<ref name="reur1">{{Cite news |date=2022-03-20 |title=Ramos-Horta, Guterres early frontrunners in East Timor election |language=en |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/ramos-horta-guterres-early-frontrunners-east-timor-election-2022-03-20/ |access-date=2022-03-21 |archive-date=21 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220321004041/https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/ramos-horta-guterres-early-frontrunners-east-timor-election-2022-03-20/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Ramos-Horta won the runoff with 62.1% of the total votes cast.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2022-04-20 |title=Timor-Leste presidential election: José Ramos-Horta wins in landslide |language=en |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/apr/21/timor-leste-presidential-election-jose-ramos-horta-wins-in-landslide |access-date=2022-04-20 |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421074707/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/apr/21/timor-leste-presidential-election-jose-ramos-horta-wins-in-landslide |url-status=live}}</ref>

The election marked the first time that someone had been re-elected to the East Timorese presidency (albeit to a non-consecutive term), as well as the second time that an incumbent president had been defeated – after the [[2012 East Timorese presidential election|2012]] election, when Ramos-Horta was eliminated in the first round.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2012/3/18/east-timor-president-out-of-poll-race | title=East Timor president out of poll race }}</ref> As a result of constitutional provisions which limit presidents to two five-year terms in a lifetime, Ramos-Horta will be ineligible to run in any future presidential election.<ref>[http://timor-leste.gov.tl/wp-content/uploads/2010/03/Constitution_RDTL_ENG.pdf "Constitution of the Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste", Section 75.3: "The President of the Republic's term of office may be renewed only once."]</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Timor-Leste]] is elected using the [[two-round system]].<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2231/ Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171020135906/http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2231/ |date=20 October 2017 }} IFES</ref> Formally, presidential candidates run as independents. However, they commonly receive the backing of political parties. In this election, Ramos-Horta was backed by the [[National Congress for Timorese Reconstruction]] (CNRT), and Guterres by [[Fretilin]].<ref name="lowy 2022-01-24">{{cite web |last1=Leach |first1=Michael |title=Timor-Leste: comebacks and contests ahead of presidential elections |url=https://www.lowyinstitute.org/the-interpreter/timor-leste-comebacks-and-contests-ahead-presidential-elections |website=The Interpreter |publisher=Lowy Institute |access-date=3 January 2025 |language=en |date=24 January 2022 |archive-date=23 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220323032438/https://www.lowyinstitute.org/the-interpreter/timor-leste-comebacks-and-contests-ahead-presidential-elections |url-status=live}}</ref> With no single candidate in the first round getting over 50% of the vote, a second round was held a month after the first with the top two candidates.<ref name="reur1"/>

==Campaign==
The campaign was described as "largely peaceful and competitive" by EU electoral observer Ruiz Devesa.<ref name="AFPTG">{{cite news |title=Timor-Leste presidential election: José Ramos-Horta wins in landslide |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/apr/21/timor-leste-presidential-election-jose-ramos-horta-wins-in-landslide |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=the Guardian |agency=Agence France-Presse |date=20 April 2022 |language=en |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421074707/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/apr/21/timor-leste-presidential-election-jose-ramos-horta-wins-in-landslide |url-status=live }}</ref> Younger voters, with less strong regional convictions and party affiliations, were considered a pivotal voter bloc during the election, being seen as contributing to the third-place finish of KHUNTO candidate [[Armanda Berta dos Santos]].<ref name="llchen">{{cite news |last1=Chen |first1=Li-Li |title=Observations From the First Round of Timor-Leste's Presidential Election |url=https://thediplomat.com/2022/03/observations-from-the-first-round-of-timor-lestes-presidential-election/ |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=The Diplomat |date=23 March 2022 |archive-date=13 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220413070733/https://thediplomat.com/2022/03/observations-from-the-first-round-of-timor-lestes-presidential-election/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Incumbent President Francisco Guterres was backed by [[Mari Alkatiri]]. Ramos-Horta's campaign was supported by [[Xanana Gusmão]], who was dubbed the "Kingmaker of Timor Leste".<ref name="AFPTG" /><ref name="aj21apr" /> The campaign between Guterres and Ramos-Horta was seen by some analysts in the [[Lowy Institute]] as a conflict between Alkatiri and Gusmao, who backed opposing candidates.<ref name="novak">{{cite news |last1=Novak |first1=Parker |title=The not-so-hidden contest behind Timor-Leste's presidential election |url=https://www.lowyinstitute.org/the-interpreter/not-so-hidden-contest-behind-timor-leste-s-presidential-election |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=Lowy Institute |date=18 April 2022 |language=en |archive-date=17 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220417222417/https://www.lowyinstitute.org/the-interpreter/not-so-hidden-contest-behind-timor-leste-s-presidential-election |url-status=live }}</ref> Guterres' previous administration was considered by outside sources to be in political deadlock since 2017.<ref name="llchen" /> Guterres was perceived to be running a competitive campaign despite his first round result, where he placed second.<ref name="novak" /> However, former Fretilin member and fourth place candidate [[Lere Anan Timur]] refused to back Guterres or Ramos-Horta.<ref name="novak" /> Fretilin ran a campaign stating that Ramos-Horta was unfit to be president, blaming him for [[2006 East Timorese crisis|the political violence and crisis]] that happened during his prime ministership in 2006.<ref name="aptd28">{{cite news |title=Timor-Leste Set for Presidential Runoff Election Next Month |url=https://thediplomat.com/2022/03/timor-leste-set-for-presidential-runoff-election-next-month/ |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=The Diplomat |agency=Associated Press |date=28 March 2022 |archive-date=4 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220404141815/https://thediplomat.com/2022/03/timor-leste-set-for-presidential-runoff-election-next-month/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
[[File:2022 Wahlkampf José Ramos-Horta 1.png|thumb|Supporters of Ramos-Horta at a rally]]
Former President José Ramos-Horta came out of retirement as he stated that incumbent president Francisco “Lu-Olo” Guterres had violated the constitution.<ref name="AFPTG" /> Guterres had previously refused to swear in several ministers from Ramos-Horta's party on the grounds that they were currently undergoing legal investigations over corruption.<ref name="aj20apr">{{cite news |title=Ramos-Horta takes commanding lead in East Timor presidential vote |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/20/east-timor-ramos-horta-takes-strong-lead-in-presidential-vote |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=Al Jazeera |date=20 April 2022 |language=en |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421074709/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/20/east-timor-ramos-horta-takes-strong-lead-in-presidential-vote |url-status=live }}</ref> Ramos-Horta stated that in the event of winning the presidential election, he would potentially dissolve parliament and call for new elections.<ref name="AFPTG" /><ref name="aj20apr" /> His support from CNRT had been secured on the basis that, amongst other things, he "... , if elected, must restore constitutional order and exercise constitutional powers and dissolve parliament and call early elections."<ref name="lowy 2022-01-24"/><ref name="rtp 2022-01-23">{{cite news |author1=<!--not stated--> |title=CNRT quer que Ramos-Horta dissolva parlamento timorense se for eleito PR |trans-title=CNRT wants Ramos-Horta to dissolve Timorese parliament if elected president |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/mundo/cnrt-quer-que-ramos-horta-dissolva-parlamento-timorense-se-for-eleito-pr_n1378900 |access-date=3 January 2025 |work=[[Rádio e Televisão de Portugal|RTP Notícias]] |agency=[[Lusa News Agency|Lusa]] |date=23 January 2022 |language=pt}}</ref><ref name="top 2022-06-28">{{cite news |last1=Oki |first1=Raimundos |title=Kalbuadi: CNRT will not force PR Horta to dissolve parliament |url=https://www.oekusipost.com/en/politics/1424-kalbuadi-cnrt-will-not-force-pr-horta-to-dissolve-parliament |access-date=3 January 2025 |work=The Oekusi Post |date=28 June 2022 |language=en-gb}}</ref> Ramos-Horta ran on a platform of poverty reduction, increasing healthcare services for mothers and children, as well as increasing job creation.<ref name="aj21apr" /> He also stated that he wanted to try and improve communication across the governing political parties for the purposes of increasing stability.<ref name="aj21apr">{{cite news |title=Ramos-Horta declares victory in East Timor presidential election |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/21/ramos-horta-declares-victory-in-east-timor-presidential-election |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=Al Jazeera |date=21 April 2022 |language=en |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421074710/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/21/ramos-horta-declares-victory-in-east-timor-presidential-election |url-status=live }}</ref> In addition, Ramos-Horta stated his intention on working with the government to address supply chain issues from the ongoing [[COVID-19 pandemic in Timor-Leste|COVID-19 pandemic]] and [[Russian invasion of Ukraine]].<ref name="reu21ap">{{cite news |title=Ramos-Horta declares victory in East Timor presidential election |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/ramos-horta-declares-victory-east-timor-presidential-election-2022-04-21/ |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=Reuters |date=21 April 2022 |language=en |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421074711/https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/ramos-horta-declares-victory-east-timor-presidential-election-2022-04-21/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Conduct==
Andrew Jacobs, the representative of the [[European Union]] in Timor-Leste, said an EU delegation from Brussels would be involved in the process of observation for the 2022 election.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sousa|first=Camilio de|title=The EU's delegation will Observe TL Presidential election process in march, 2022|url=http://www.tatoli.tl/en/2021/09/18/the-eus-delegation-will-observe-tl-presidential-election-process-in-march-2022/|access-date=2022-01-11|language=en-US|archive-date=11 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220111172742/http://www.tatoli.tl/en/2021/09/18/the-eus-delegation-will-observe-tl-presidential-election-process-in-march-2022/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The representative of the [[United States]] embassy in Dili, Charge d’Affaires Tom Daley, said the U.S. would send observers to oversee the election process. He added that the United States "has supported Timor-Leste in developing democracy since Timor-Leste's independence" and that the U.S. had plans to send observers to ensure the transparency of the election and the freedom of voters to be able "to choose the candidates for the president without intimidation from any party".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sousa|first=Camilio de|title=The U.S. will send observers to assist the 2022 presidential election|url=http://www.tatoli.tl/en/2021/11/05/the-u-s-will-send-observers-to-assist-the-2022-presidential-election/|access-date=2022-01-11|language=en-US|archive-date=11 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220111172738/http://www.tatoli.tl/en/2021/11/05/the-u-s-will-send-observers-to-assist-the-2022-presidential-election/|url-status=live}}</ref>

